From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: show GL frames as IOSurfaces

With gl=on the window's CAOpenGLLayer drew the guest's scanout on the
main thread, inside drawInCGLContext, holding the BQL: every frame the
main thread waited for QEMU's lock and QEMU waited for the main thread.

Draw the scanout into one of three IOSurfaces instead, on QEMU's thread
with a context of its own, and show that surface as the layer's contents.
A serial queue waits for the GPU to finish a surface before Core Animation
gets it (testing the fence and napping 100 us, as Apple's
glClientWaitSync would spin a core); a surface is reused only once Core
Animation no longer uses it (IOSurfaceIsInUse). The main thread no longer
takes the BQL to draw. Core Animation composites the surface directly. At
most one surface is drawn per display refresh (a timer catches up with the
newest frame), so a guest that flushes thousands of times a second costs
no more than that.

The guest sets the scanout size, so it must not set the Mac's memory use:
the surfaces are capped at the largest display's pixels (a larger scanout
is drawn scaled down; no window can show more), and they are made again
at most twice a second. A guest that switches between two sizes on every
frame gets its frames scaled into the surfaces it has, instead of three
new surfaces per frame. Before, the only cap was 16384x16384 (3 x 1 GiB).

OMACVM_GL_PRESENT=layer goes back to the CAOpenGLLayer path (GPU safe mode
sets it); if the IOSurface contexts cannot be made, the window falls back
to it as well. The log says which path the window took.
Applies after qemu-cocoa-gl-present-on-flush.

Upstream: not sent (changes how QEMU's Cocoa display draws).
Test: a capture of the window matches the guest's grim (upright, colours);
a guest switching between two resolutions in a loop keeps QEMU's memory
flat; 30-minute soaks; GPU safe mode A/B in the app.

